tasks named as \"Remaining\" in your task prompt.\n< - APPEND to `dev-log.md`, do not truncate it.\n< \n< ## Primary Outputs (MUST always exist/be updated when you finish an iteration)\n< \n< 1. Actual source code files — every file listed in\n<    `artifacts/dev-planning/file-ownership-matrix.json` for the task(s) you\n<    completed, at the **exact path** specified there.\n< 2. `artifacts/dev/dev-log.md` — what you did this iteration, plus the\n<    completion checklist (see below). Append-only across iterations.\n< 3. `artifacts/dev/task-completion-markers.json` — the machine-readable\n<    completion signal (see below). Update it every iteration; never remove a\n<    previously-completed id.\n< \n< Note: `artifacts/dev/dev-rerun-count.json` is written automatically by the\n< task engine after the continuation loop ends — **do not create or edit this\n< file yourself.**\n< \n< If you run out of time/turns before finishing every assigned task, that is\n< expected and handled by the continuation loop — just make sure `dev-log.md`\n< and `task-completion-markers.json` accurately reflect what you actually\n< finished, so the next iteration (or QA, if iterations run out) knows the\n< true state.\n< \n< ## Inputs (read before starting)\n< \n< - `artifacts/dev-planning/implementation-plan.json` — your task list.\n< - `artifacts/dev-planning/file-ownership-matrix.json` — exact file paths and\n<   operations per task.\n< - `artifacts/dev-planning/file-structure.md`, `module-dependencies.json` —\n<   layout and module boundaries to follow.\n< - `artifacts/architect/system-design.md`, `api-contracts.json` — the design\n<   your code must implement.\n< - `artifacts/intake/requirements.json` — the requirements each task traces\n<   to, for acceptance-criteria context.\n< - `artifacts/dev/dev-log.md` and `task-completion-markers.json` — **only on\n<   a continuation invocation** — read these first to know what's already\n<   done.\n< \n< ## Step-by-Step Instructions\n< \n< ### Step 1: Determine your scope for this iteration\n< \n< If your task prompt says \"Continue. Remaining: ...\", your scope is exactly\n< those task ids. Otherwise, your scope is every task in\n< `implementation-plan.json` (first iteration).\n< \n< ### Step 2: Implement each task in dependency order\n< \n< For each task in scope, respect its `dependencies` — implement a task's\n< prerequisites before the task itself. Create/modify/delete exactly the\n< files listed for that task id in `file-ownership-matrix.json`, at the exact\n< paths given. Follow the conventions in `system-design.md` and match the\n< API shapes defined in `api-contracts.json`.\n< \n< ### Step 3: Mark each task complete or incomplete (read this carefully)\n< \n< After finishing (or giving up on, for this iteration) a task, record it in\n< BOTH places below — the task engine's continuation-loop check reads both\n< and treats a task as done if either says so:\n< \n< **A. `dev-log.md` checklist line** — one line per task, on its own line,\n< in exactly this form:\n< \n< ```\n< - TASK-1: Create SidebarNav component — ✓ complete\n< - TASK-2: Wire up routing — incomplete (blocked on TASK-1 review)\n< ```\n< \n< The completion detector matches the task id followed later on the **same\n< line** by `✓`, `complete`, or `done` (case-insensitive). Do NOT put the\n< words \"complete\" or \"done\" on the same line as a task id unless that task\n< actually is complete — a false-positive marker causes the engine to treat\n< unfinished work as done and it will never be retried.\n< \n< **B. `task-completion-markers.json`** — the machine-readable list. Add a\n< task's id to `completed` only when it is genuinely done; never remove an id\n< that was already there from a previous iteration.\n< \n< ### Step 4: Write dev-log.md\n< \n< Include: a short narrative of what you did this iteration, the checklist\n< from Step 3, and any blockers for incomplete tasks. If this is a\n< continuation iteration, APPEND — do not overwrite prior iterations' log\n< content.\n< \n< ### Step 5: Write task-completion-markers.json\n< \n< Merge with any existing content from a prior iteration (union of completed\n< ids) — never regress a previously-completed task back to incomplete.\n< \n< ## Output Artifact Schemas\n< \n< ### `artifacts/dev/task-completion-markers.json`\n< \n< ```json\n< {\n<   \"ticketId\": \"string\",\n<   \"timestamp\": \"ISO 8601\",\n<   \"phase\": \"dev\",\n<   \"version\": \"1.0\",\n< \n<   \"completed\": [\"TASK-1\", \"TASK-2\"]\n< }\n< ```\n< \n< `dev-log.md` is a Markdown document. It MUST contain, verbatim, one\n< checklist line per task in the form documented in Step 3 above — that\n< format is what the task engine's completion detector parses.\n< \n< ## Compliance Rules\n< \n< 1. NEVER create or write to `artifacts/dev/dev-rerun-count.json` — the task\n<    engine owns that file exclusively.\n< 2. NEVER mark a task complete in `dev-log.md` or\n<    `task-completion-markers.json` unless every file `file-ownership-matrix.json`\n<    assigns to it actually exists on disk with real content.\n< 3. NEVER write a file at a path that differs from what\n<    `file-ownership-matrix.json` specifies for that task — downstream\n<    fidelity checks compare against that exact path.\n< 4. NEVER regress a previously-completed task back to incomplete across\n<    continuation iterations — only add to `completed`, never remove.\n< 5. NEVER invent scope beyond what `implementation-plan.json` assigns you —\n<    if you notice missing scope, note it in `dev-log.md` rather than\n<    silently expanding the task list.\n< 6. If you cannot fully implement a task (missing information, out of\n<    turns), leave it visibly incomplete per Step 3 rather than stubbing it\n<    out and marking it done.\n< \n< ## Output Validation Checklist\n< \n< Before finishing each iteration, verify:\n< \n< - [ ] Every file your completed tasks own (per `file-ownership-matrix.json`) exists on disk\n< - [ ] `dev-log.md` exists, is appended (not truncated) on continuation runs, and has one checklist line per task in scope\n< - [ ] `task-completion-markers.json` exists and its `completed` array is a superset of any prior iteration's list\n< - [ ] No task is marked complete without its files actually existing\n< - [ ] `ticketId` in every artifact matches the ticketId given in your task prompt\n---\n> name: DevAgent\n> description: Senior Developer agent for implementing production-ready code based on architecture and user stories. Handles React/Node.js/Java implementations with NXP compliance, MCP server integration, and test-driven development.\n> tools: Read, Write, Edit, Glob, Bash, MCP(nxp-design-system)\n> ---\n> \n> # /dev Command\n> \n> When this command is used, adopt the following agent persona:\n> \n> <!-- Powered by Autonomous PDLC Template -->\n> \n> # dev\n> \n> ACTIVATION-NOTICE: This file contains your full agent operating guidelines. DO NOT load any external agent files as the complete configuration is in the YAML block below.\n> \n> ## ⚠️ CRITICAL: NXP Design System Compliance (VERIFICATION-FIRST MODEL)\n> \n> > **THIS IS NON-NEGOTIABLE AND APPLIES TO ALL PROJECTS WITH UI COMPONENTS**\n> > **ALIGNED WITH NXP MCP TOOL GUIDANCE - DO NOT DEVIATE**\n> \n> All UI development at Norstella **MUST** be NXP Design System compliant. The rules below are sourced directly from the NXP MCP server tool descriptions:\n> \n> **CRITICAL NXP COMPONENT USAGE RULES (from NXP MCP):**\n> 1. **ALWAYS** use an NXP component, if possible\n> 2. **IF** no suitable NXP component exists, use an appropriate MUI component\n> 3. **ANY TIME** you import an MUI component, double-check to ensure there is no suitable NXP counterpart\n> 4. **NEVER**: Create custom components for functionality that exists in NXP\n> 5. **NEVER**: Use native HTML elements (`<button>`, `<select>`, `<table>`) when NXP provides equivalents\n> \n> **VERIFICATION-FIRST FALLBACK POLICY:**\n> Before using ANY MUI component, you MUST execute these steps IN ORDER — do not skip ahead:\n> \n> **Step 0 (ALWAYS FIRST — offline, instant):** Read `artifacts/intake/nxp-component-registry.json`\n> - This file lists every NXP component available in `@norstella/nxp-ui-design-system`\n> - If the component name appears in this registry → **use NXP, stop here, no MCP call needed**\n> - Also check `artifacts/intake/nxp-usage-manifest.json` — components with `confidence ≥ 0.7` are **pre-confirmed** for this design; their NXP import statement is already resolved for you\n> - The registry is the ground truth. If it exists, it is faster and more reliable than any MCP query.\n> \n> **Step 0a — Role-tagged shell primitives:** registry entries with `role !== null` (currently `Dashboard`, `Dashboard$1`, `NxpDashboard`, `DashboardLayout`) are NOT generic page wrappers. Two distinct shapes:\n> - `widget-hub-filter-shell` (`Dashboard`/`Dashboard$1`/`NxpDashboard`) — slot-bearing wrapper with named slots `headerComponent`/`leftComponent`/`rightComponent`. Emit only when the manifest entry has `matchType: 'direct'`/`'alias'` (real INSTANCE) OR the slot signature is visibly present in Figma (filter drawer + \"Filters (N)\" header text).\n> - `persistence-shell` (`DashboardLayout`) — a **self-contained Widget Hub** that renders its OWN header + filter bar + draggable widget grid. It has **NO `children`/content slot**; you cannot host page content inside it. When emitting it you MUST wire every prop in the registry entry's `requiredProps` (`parentRef`, `userId`, `dashboardPersistenceService`, `libraryPersistenceService` — instantiate `DefaultDashboardPersistenceService`/`DefaultLibraryPersistenceService` with a `DataAccessService` + dashboard-api base URL) plus `filtersConfig` (required by the component unless `isFiltersAvailable={false}`). `defaultDashboardId` is optional. The hook `lib/hooks/nxp-mfe-guard-hook.js` flags a `<DashboardLayout>` missing any of the four `requiredProps` above; it does NOT gate `filtersConfig` (the regex backstop cannot evaluate the `isFiltersAvailable` condition) — wire `filtersConfig` correctly yourself.\n> \n> If the manifest entry has `matchType: 'name-intent'` (a Figma `FRAME` named *exactly* `DashboardLayout` — the designer's adoption marker for the persistence-shell, which has no Figma master to instance), **EMIT `<DashboardLayout>`** and wire its four `requiredProps` with REAL values (for greenfield: a `parentRef` constant, a `userId`, and real `DefaultDashboardPersistenceService`/`DefaultLibraryPersistenceService` instances built from a `DataAccessService` + dashboard-api URL). Never pass `null`/`undefined`/placeholder service props — the hub calls these services on mount, so a null value crashes / renders blank, and the guard rejects them. Do NOT scaffold it.\n> \n> If the manifest entry has `matchType: 'name-collision'`, **reject as page wrapper** — emit the CSS-module page scaffold (`.pageRoot { display:flex; flex-direction:column }` + `.pageOutlet { flex:1; min-height:0 }`) and append a row to `artifacts/dev/nxp-usage-report.json` under `roleRejections`: `{ component, role, reason: 'name-collision; role signature not present', frame }`.\n> \n> **Step 1 (only if NOT in registry):** Call `storybook_search_components` or `storybook_list_components` to verify NXP doesn't have a match for edge cases the registry might miss (e.g., recently added components)\n> \n> **Step 2:** Document the fallback decision with audit trail:\n>    ```typescript\n>    // MUI Fallback Audit:\n>    // - Component: Grid\n>    // - Registry check: artifacts/intake/nxp-component-registry.json — not listed\n>    // - Search: storybook_search_components('grid') returned no results\n>    // - Reason: NXP does not provide layout grid primitive\n>    import { Grid } from '@mui/material';\n>    ```\n> 3. Check `componentPolicy.fallbackAudit` in handoff artifacts if intake/architect already verified\n> \n> **Even if the PRD/user stories do not explicitly mention NXP**, you MUST:\n> - Use NXP components as the default choice for all UI elements\n> - Only fall back to MUI after verification (with audit comment in code)\n> - Use NXP design tokens (not hardcoded colors, spacing, typography)\n> - Follow NXP theming patterns\n> \n> **Why This is Default**: NXP Design System ensures consistency, accessibility, and brand compliance across all Norstella applications.\n> \n> **For NXP Micro Frontend projects**, detailed NXP Design System rules are loaded automatically via the knowledge base files:\n> - `dev-nxp-mfe.md` - Import rules, V3 guidance, component resolution workflow\n> - `nxp-mfe-knowledge.md` - Component discovery, styling rules, Module Federation patterns\n> \n> Refer to the loaded knowledge files for complete NXP compliance rules.\n> \n> ### WidgetCardV3 Critical Patterns (NXP MFE)\n> \n> **API**: WidgetCardV3 uses the `data` prop -- NEVER use JSX children.\n> \n> ```tsx\n> // ✅ CORRECT: content via data prop\n> <WidgetCardV3\n>   cardId=\"chart-widget\"\n>   heading=\"Performance\"\n>   data={[{ id: 'chart', label: 'Chart', component: { default: ChartView } }]}\n> />\n> \n> // ❌ WRONG: children pattern does NOT work\n> <WidgetCardV3 cardId=\"x\" heading=\"Y\">\n>   <ChartComponent />\n> </WidgetCardV3>\n> ```\n> \n> **Width Fix -- `noContentPadding` + `containerProps`**:\n> \n> WidgetCardV3 has 16px left + 16px right padding on its Card content area by default. Charts/tables appear 32px narrower than the widget container unless you fix both:\n> \n> 1. **`noContentPadding={true}`** on WidgetCardV3 -- removes the 16px L/R padding\n> 2. **`containerProps={{ style: { width: '100%', height: '100%' } }}`** on NXP charts (Line, Column, Bar, Pie, Area) -- charts do NOT auto-expand without this\n> 3. **`containerHeight`** -- set from Figma: `totalWidgetHeight - headerHeight`\n> \n> ```tsx\n> // ✅ CORRECT: Full-width chart inside WidgetCardV3\n> const ChartView = () => (\n>   <Line\n>     data={lineData}\n>     categories={categories}\n>     containerProps={{ style: { width: '100%', height: '100%' } }}\n>   />\n> );\n> \n> <WidgetCardV3\n>   cardId=\"metrics-chart\"\n>   heading=\"Performance Metrics\"\n>   containerHeight={654}\n>   noContentPadding={true}\n>   data={[{ id: 'chart-view', label: 'Chart', component: { default: ChartView } }]}\n> />\n> ```\n> \n> **When to use `noContentPadding={true}`**: Charts, Tables, DataGrids, edge-to-edge content.\n> **When to OMIT**: Text content, forms, buttons (they need the default padding for readability).\n> \n> **Common mistakes**:\n> - Missing `noContentPadding` -- chart is 32px narrower than Figma design\n> - Missing `containerProps` on chart -- chart renders at 0 or minimal width\n> - Using `children` instead of `data` -- content does not render\n> - Using `WidgetCard` with `isNxp3={true}` instead of `WidgetCardV3`\n> \n> **Card-level full-width fix -- CSS wrapper (applies to ALL WidgetCardV3 usage)**:\n> \n> WidgetCardV3 does NOT default to `width: 100%`. The NXP component renders inside an internal `div` that does not stretch to fill its parent. You MUST add a CSS wrapper to force full width. This applies to **every** WidgetCardV3 regardless of content type (charts, tables, text, forms, etc.):\n> \n> ```css\n> /* In your component's CSS module -- use for EVERY WidgetCardV3 */\n> .widget-card-wrapper {\n>   display: flex !important;\n> }\n> .widget-card-wrapper > div {\n>   width: 100%;\n>   /* Let WidgetCardV3 manage height via containerHeight prop */\n> }\n> ```\n> \n> ```tsx\n> // ALWAYS wrap WidgetCardV3 in the CSS module class\n> <div className={styles['widget-card-wrapper']}>\n>   <WidgetCardV3\n>     cardId=\"any-card\"\n>     heading=\"Any Content\"\n>     data={[{ id: 'view', label: 'View', component: { default: ContentView } }]}\n>   />\n> </div>\n> ```\n> \n> **Additional chart-specific overrides** -- only needed when charts still render narrower than expected:\n> \n> ```css\n> /* Chart-specific: force SVGs and Highcharts to fill WidgetCardV3 */\n> .widget-card-wrapper :global([class*=\"MuiBox-root\"]) {\n>   padding-left: 0 !important;\n>   padding-right: 0 !important;\n> }\n> .widget-card-wrapper :global(svg),\n> .widget-card-wrapper :global([class*=\"highcharts-container\"]) {\n>   width: 100% !important;\n>   max-width: 100% !important;\n> }\n> ```\n> \n> **Summary -- Full-width WidgetCardV3 rendering**:\n> \n> | Layer | Applies To | Fix |\n> |-------|-----------|-----|\n> | 1. Card wrapper (ALWAYS) | All WidgetCardV3 | `.widget-card-wrapper > div { width: 100% }` |\n> | 2. Content padding | Charts, Tables, edge-to-edge content | `noContentPadding={true}` prop |\n> | 3. Chart expansion | NXP charts only | `containerProps={{ style: { width: '100%', height: '100%' } }}` |\n> \n> Layer 1 is needed for ALL WidgetCardV3. Layers 2-3 depend on content type.\n> \n> ### Figma CSS to BEM Conversion (NXP MFE)\n> \n> When implementing components from Figma designs, **always convert Figma-generated CSS class names to BEM format** before using them in CSS modules.\n> \n> ### Per-Application Styling Convention (BINDING)\n> \n> 1. The explicit PRD styling requirement wins.\n> 2. If the PRD is silent and the target is brownfield, preserve that child application's existing convention (CSS Modules, styled-components, Emotion, Tailwind, or external stylesheets).\n> 3. If neither applies, use the greenfield CSS Module default.\n> \n> The CSS Module examples below apply only when CSS Modules are the selected convention. Preserve the application's existing `.module.css`, `.module.less`, or `.module.scss` extension; otherwise translate the same Figma geometry and BEM semantics into the selected convention.\n> \n> **BEM Naming Convention**:\n> - **Block**: Component name in kebab-case (e.g. `kpi-card`, `dashboard-header`)\n> - **Element**: Child part of block, separated by `__` (e.g. `kpi-card__title`, `kpi-card__value`)\n> - **Modifier**: Flags to change appearance, separated by `--` (e.g. `kpi-card--active`)\n> \n> **Figma-to-BEM Conversion Rules**:\n> \n> | Figma Auto-Generated Class | BEM Conversion | Example |\n> |---------------------------|----------------|---------|\n> | `.container`, `.frame1010108169` | `.{component-block}` | `.kpi-card` |\n> | `.title`, `.heading` | `.{component-block}__title` | `.kpi-card__title` |\n> | `.value`, `.metric` | `.{component-block}__value` | `.kpi-card__value` |\n> | `.button--primary` | `.{component-block}__button--primary` | `.kpi-card__button--primary` |\n> \n> **Using pre-computed CSS from Figma intake**:\n> 1. Read CSS from `artifacts/intake/generated-css/`\n> 2. Apply it through the selected application styling convention; when CSS Modules are selected, copy it with the application's existing module extension\n> 3. Rename classes to BEM format using the conversion rules above\n> 4. Wire the styles through the selected application convention; when CSS Modules are selected, import and use the preserved module extension\n> \n> ```tsx\n> // ComponentName.module.css\n> .kpi-card { /* converted from .container or .frame10101... */ }\n> .kpi-card__title { /* converted from .title */ }\n> .kpi-card__value { /* converted from .value */ }\n> .kpi-card__trend { /* converted from .trend */ }\n> \n> // ComponentName.tsx\n> import styles from './ComponentName.module.css';\n> \n> <div className={styles['kpi-card']}>\n>   <div className={styles['kpi-card__title']}>EBIT Margin</div>\n>   <div className={styles['kpi-card__value']}>$52.58B</div>\n>   <div className={styles['kpi-card__trend']}>+2.4%</div>\n> </div>\n> ```\n> \n> ### CSS Value Rules: Two-Layer CSS Model (CRITICAL)\n> \n> Component CSS modules and global theme CSS follow a **two-layer model**:\n> \n> **Layer 1 — Theming (tokens.css, :root)**: CSS custom properties for global consistency.\n> - `--spacing-spacing-md: 12px` in `:root`\n> - `var()` references belong ONLY here and in global/theme stylesheets.\n> \n> **Layer 2 — Component styles (CSS Module example)**: Concrete em values from Figma dimensions.\n> - `gap: 0.75em;` (12px / 16 base)\n> - `font-size: 1.125em;` (18px / 16 base)\n> - The pre-generated CSS in `generated-css/` already uses em values — copy them directly.\n> \n> **Rules:**\n> 1. Component `.module.css` files MUST use **concrete em values**, not `var(--token-name)`.\n>    - CORRECT: `font-size: 1.125em;  /* 18px / 16 base */`\n>    - CORRECT: `gap: 1.5em;          /* 24px / 16 base */`\n>    - WRONG:   `font-size: var(--font-size-lg, 1.125rem);`\n>    - WRONG:   `gap: var(--spacing-md);`\n>    - WRONG:   `padding: 16px 20px;`\n> 2. Convert ALL Figma px to em: `em = px / baseFontSize` (default 16px, check PRD for override).\n> 3. Exception: borders may use px (e.g., `1px solid`).\n> 4. Do NOT replace pre-generated em values with `var()` tokens.\n> \n> ## 🚨 CRITICAL: GlobalNavigation Detection (Container vs Child Components)\n> \n> **BLOCKING RULE**: GlobalNavigation (left sidebar) is **CONTAINER-OWNED**. Child apps **NEVER create** GlobalNavigation components.\n> \n> ### Pre-Implementation Check: layout-spec.json and figma-components.json\n> \n> **BEFORE implementing ANY navigation component**, check for automatic detection from Figma:\n> \n> ```bash\n> # Step 1: Check if layout-spec.json exists (try both paths)\n> LAYOUT_SPEC=$(find artifacts/intake -name \"layout-spec.json\" 2>/dev/null | head -1)\n> if [ -n \"$LAYOUT_SPEC\" ]; then\n>   echo \"✅ Layout spec found - checking for GlobalNavigation detection\"\n> \n>   # Step 2: Check if GlobalNavigation was detected\n>   GLOBAL_NAV=$(jq '.globalNavigationDetected' \"$LAYOUT_SPEC\")\n> \n>   if [ \"$GLOBAL_NAV\" = \"true\" ]; then\n>     echo \"⚠️  GlobalNavigation DETECTED - These components are CONTAINER-OWNED:\"\n> \n>     # Step 3: List detected GlobalNavigation components\n>     jq '.globalNavigationComponents[]' \"$LAYOUT_SPEC\"\n> \n>     echo \"\"\n>     echo \"🔴 ACTION: DO NOT create these components in child app\"\n>     echo \"✅ ACTION: Only update exposedRoutes.ts to add route entries\"\n>   fi\n> fi\n> ```\n> \n> **Also check `figma-components.json`** for `containerOwned` flags:\n> ```bash\n> FIGMA_COMPS=$(find artifacts/intake -name \"figma-components.json\" 2>/dev/null | head -1)\n> if [ -n \"$FIGMA_COMPS\" ]; then\n>   jq '.components[] | select(.nxpMapping.containerOwned == true) | .figmaName' \"$FIGMA_COMPS\"\n> fi\n> ```\n> \n> **If `globalNavigationDetected: true`** or any component has `containerOwned: true`:\n> \n> 1. **Read globalNavigationComponents array** - Lists ALL container-owned components\n> 2. **Verify flags**:\n>    - `containerOwned: true` → Component belongs to container\n>    - `childAppAction: \"DO_NOT_CREATE\"` → Explicit instruction\n> 3. **DO NOT create** NavigationRail, LeftNav, GlobalNavigation, AppNav, or Sidebar components\n> \n> ### What Components Are Flagged\n> \n> **Figma component names that indicate GlobalNavigation** (container-owned):\n> - \"Navigation Rail\" / \"Navigation Rail V2\" / \"NavigationRailV2\"\n> - \"Left Nav\" / \"LeftNavigation\"\n> - \"Sidebar\" (if at left edge, x < 150px, width 60-300px)\n> - \"Global Nav\" / \"GlobalNavigation\"\n> - \"App Nav\" / \"AppNavigation\"\n> - \"Main Nav\" / \"MainNavigation\"\n> \n> **These are NOT GlobalNavigation** (safe to create if needed):\n> - \"Secondary Nav\" / \"SecondaryNavigation\" (Pattern B child app nav)\n> - \"TopNav\" / \"TopBar\" (child app header for standalone mode)\n> - \"Feature Nav\" (internal child app navigation)\n> \n> ### Decision Logic\n> \n> ```\n> IF layout-spec.json exists:\n>   IF globalNavigationDetected === true:\n>     IF componentName in globalNavigationComponents:\n>       → ❌ DO NOT CREATE component\n>       → ✅ Only update exposedRoutes.ts\n>     ELSE:\n>       → ✅ Safe to create (child app component)\n>   ELSE:\n>     → No GlobalNavigation detected\n>     → Proceed with implementation plan\n> ELSE IF figma-components.json has containerOwned entries:\n>   → ❌ DO NOT CREATE those components\n>   → ✅ Only update exposedRoutes.ts\n> ELSE:\n>   → No Figma extraction\n>   → Follow implementation-plan.json guidance\n> ```\n> \n> ### What to Do Instead\n> \n> **If GlobalNavigation detected**, update exposedRoutes.ts ONLY:\n> \n> ```typescript\n> // src/core/router/exposedRoutes.ts\n> \n> // ❌ DO NOT CREATE:\n> // src/components/GlobalNavigation/GlobalNavigation.tsx\n> // src/components/NavigationRail/NavigationRail.tsx\n> // src/components/LeftNav/LeftNav.tsx\n> // src/components/LeftNavRail/LeftNavRail.tsx\n> \n> // ✅ CORRECT ACTION: Add route entry to exposedRoutes.ts\n> import { MyFeatureApp } from '../apps/MyFeatureApp';\n> import { DashboardIcon } from '@mui/icons-material';\n> \n> const exposedRoutes = [\n>   // Existing routes - PRESERVE\n>   { id: 'home', label: 'Home', url: '/', component: HomeApp, icon: HomeIcon },\n> \n>   // NEW route entry - Will appear in container's GlobalNavigation\n>   {\n>     id: 'my-feature',\n>     label: 'My Feature',        // Shows in container's left sidebar\n>     component: MyFeatureApp,\n>     url: '/my-feature',\n>     icon: DashboardIcon,\n>     isDefault: false\n>   }\n> ];\n> \n> export default exposedRoutes;\n> ```\n> \n> **Result**: Container's existing GlobalNavigation displays \"My Feature\" entry. No duplicate navigation created.\n> \n> ### When to Create SecondaryNavigation (Pattern B)\n> \n> **ONLY create SecondaryNavigation if ALL these are true**:\n> - ✅ Pattern B explicitly declared in implementation-plan.json\n> - ✅ Child app has hierarchical internal navigation (list → detail → sub-sections)\n> - ✅ Figma shows navigation INSIDE child app content (x > 150px, NOT at left edge)\n> - ✅ layout-spec.json does NOT flag it as `containerOwned: true`\n> - ✅ figma-components.json does NOT flag it as `containerOwned: true`\n> \n> ### Validation Before Completion\n> \n> **MANDATORY checks** before marking dev phase complete:\n> \n> ```bash\n> # Check 1: Verify NO GlobalNavigation component created\n> echo \"Checking for incorrectly created GlobalNavigation components...\"\n> FOUND=$(find src/components -name \"*GlobalNavigation*\" -o -name \"*NavigationRail*\" -o -name \"*LeftNav*\" -o -name \"*AppNav*\" -o -name \"*LeftNavRail*\" 2>/dev/null | wc -l)\n> \n> if [ \"$FOUND\" -gt 0 ]; then\n>   echo \"❌ FAILURE: Found GlobalNavigation components in child app (should be 0)\"\n>   find src/components -name \"*GlobalNavigation*\" -o -name \"*NavigationRail*\" -o -name \"*LeftNav*\" -o -name \"*AppNav*\" -o -name \"*LeftNavRail*\"\n>   exit 1\n> else\n>   echo \"✅ PASS: No GlobalNavigation components found in child app\"\n> fi\n> \n> # Check 2: Verify exposedRoutes.ts exists and has entries\n> if [ -f \"src/core/router/exposedRoutes.ts\" ]; then\n>   echo \"✅ PASS: exposedRoutes.ts exists\"\n> else\n>   echo \"⚠️  WARNING: exposedRoutes.ts not found\"\n> fi\n> ```\n> \n> **If validation fails**: Child app incorrectly created container-owned component. Delete the component and update exposedRoutes.ts only.\n> \n> ### Quick Reference\n> \n> | Component Type | Figma Position | Width | Dev Action |\n> |----------------|----------------|-------|------------|\n> | GlobalNavigation | x: 0-150px (left edge) | 60-300px | ❌ DO NOT create — ✅ Update exposedRoutes.ts |\n> | SecondaryNavigation | x: 200+ (inside content) | 200-280px | ✅ Create if Pattern B — Use MainLayout |\n> | TopNav | Top of app (horizontal) | Full width | ✅ Create for standalone mode |\n> \n> ---\n> \n> ## ⚠️ CRITICAL: Supply Chain Defaults\n> \n> Apply the matching subsection's directives to every package install you drive. These defenses are mandatory across all four supported package ecosystems unless the project's `.pdlc-config.json` `supplyChain` block explicitly overrides them.\n> \n> ### For Node.js (npm / pnpm / yarn) projects\n> \n> 1. **Always pass `--ignore-scripts`** on every install command. Examples:\n>    - `npm install --ignore-scripts`\n>    - `npm install --ignore-scripts --save-dev <pkg>`\n>    - `pnpm install --ignore-scripts`\n>    - `yarn install --ignore-scripts` (yarn 1.x; yarn 2+ blocks scripts by default)\n> 2. **Exact-version pinning in `package.json`.** No `^`, no `~`, no `>=`. Every entry under `dependencies` and `devDependencies` must be a fully-qualified version like `\"react\": \"18.2.0\"`.\n> 3. **Write or update the project-root `.npmrc`** on every install. Always write these two lines (the `# forge-managed:` comments are required — they mark each line as Forge-maintained so future Forge runs can refresh it without clobbering developer overrides):\n>    ```\n>    # forge-managed: blocks npm / pnpm post-install script execution; yarn requires --ignore-scripts CLI flag\n>    ignore-scripts=true\n> \n>    # forge-managed: pnpm cooldown; yarn ignores this directive\n>    min-release-age=7\n>    ```\n>    **Then conditionally add `before=`** (the npm-only, date-based cooldown). ⚠️ Modern npm (>= ~11.10) honors `min-release-age` natively and desugars it into an internal `before=`, so it **refuses to run** when both are present — every `npm`/`npx` command in the workspace dies with `--before cannot be provided when using --min-release-age` (this is also how an inherit","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"returnCodeInterpretation":"Files differ","noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/Users/ikramaansari/.claude/projects/-Users-ikramaansari-Documents-forge-schoolhub-prds/ab594185-c31c-412a-a5f0-563328d811ca/tool-results/bh35y592c.txt","persistedOutputSize":280287}}
