{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019hFysH5tTDETz4SG6xdXC8","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Tech Stack Decisions: PRD-08 File Storage & Course Content (SCH-008)\n2\t\n3\tTicket: SH-8 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-06T06:30:00Z · Status: complete\n4\t\n5\t**Base stack.** `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` reports `react` at 100% confidence, and the workspace confirms it: Next.js 14.2.18 App Router, React 18.3.1, TypeScript strict (`moduleResolution: bundler`), Prisma 5.20.0 on PostgreSQL 16, Auth.js v5 beta.25, Zod 3.23.8, MUI 9.5.0 (material, icons-material, material-nextjs, x-data-grid, x-date-pickers with `AdapterDateFns` already in `src/app/providers.tsx`, x-charts), @dnd-kit/core 6.3.1, date-fns/date-fns-tz, Vitest 2.1.1 (jsdom) and Playwright 1.47.2. `package.json` declares `engines.node >=18.18.0`, and local Node is 24.11.1. Nothing in the requirements calls for a different stack. The ADRs below cover how this feature fits into the existing stack and pin the new dependencies.\n6\t\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t## ADR-1: Keep the existing stack and add only the five PRD-allowed dependencies, with pinned versions\n10\t\n11\t**Context.** NFR-9 limits new dependencies to file-type, react-markdown, remark-gfm, rehype-sanitize and @dnd-kit/sortable. The latest file-type (22.x) declares `engines.node >=22` and 21.x declares `>=20`. Either would break the repo's `>=18.18.0` engine contract. All five packages are ESM-only.\n12\t\n13\t**Decision.** Install these exact pins:\n14\t| Package | Version | Why this version |\n15\t|---|---|---|\n16\t| `file-type` | `20.5.0` | Newest line with `engines.node >=18`. Use `fileTypeFromBuffer` (FR-5, FR-6). |\n17\t| `react-markdown` | `10.1.0` | Peer is `react >=18` (FR-31). |\n18\t| `remark-gfm` | `4.0.1` | Supports tables and strikethrough for FR-25 and FR-31. |\n19\t| `rehype-sanitize` | `6.0.0` | Provides NFR-2 sanitisation. |\n20\t| `@dnd-kit/sortable` | `10.0.0` | Peer is `@dnd-kit/core ^6.3.0`, which matches the installed 6.3.1 (FR-23). |\n21\t\n22\tESM-only packages import cleanly into App Router server and client code under `moduleResolution: bundler`, so `transpilePackages` is not needed. Add it only if `next build` complains.\n23\t\n24\t**Consequences.** No engine bump is needed. Do not upgrade file-type past 20.x without also raising `engines.node`. NFR-11 (single local-disk storage, no S3 SDK) means no cloud dependency is added.\n25\t\n26\t---\n27\t\n28\t## ADR-2: Parse multipart uploads with `busboy` (already installed via Next.js), not `request.formData()`\n29\t\n30\t**Context.** NFR-5 requires uploads to stream to disk, with sha256 computed and the size limit enforced during the stream. In Next 14, `Request.formData()` buffers the whole body in memory, which violates NFR-5 for 25 MB files (FR-4). `busboy@1.6.0` is already in `node_modules` as a direct dependency of `next@14.2.18` (`npm ls busboy`). Adding another parser would break NFR-9.\n31\t\n32\t**Decision.** `POST /api/files` (FR-3) runs on the Node runtime (`export const runtime = 'nodejs'`). It pipes `Readable.fromWeb(req.body)` into `busboy({ headers, limits: { files: 1, fileSize: MAX_UPLOAD_MB*1024*1024 } })`. The file stream goes through a `crypto.createHash('sha256')` tap and into `storage.saveFile()` (FR-2), which writes to a temp path under `UPLOAD_DIR` and then `rename`s it to `<yyyy>/<mm>/<cuid>.<ext>` (FR-1). Busboy's `limit` event aborts the upload, deletes the temp file and returns `VALIDATION_ERROR 'File exceeds 25 MB.'` (FR-4). Type sniffing (ADR-3) runs on the first chunk(s) before the write is committed. The `context` field must come before the file part in the multipart body because the logo rules (FR-40, FR-41) depend on it. FileUpload (FR-13) appends `context` first.\n33\t\n34\t**Consequences.** busboy is a transitive dependency, so `@types/busboy` is not available. Declare a minimal local type or accept `any` at that one call site, with a `ponytail:` comment explaining it. If a Next upgrade drops busboy, add `busboy` explicitly at that point. The intake handoff should note this as the only deviation from the dependency list, and it is a zero-install one. The rate limit check (NFR-3, `rateLimit('upload:'+userId, 60, 3_600_000)` from `src/server/rate-limit.ts`) runs before any bytes are read.\n35\t\n36\t---\n37\t\n38\t## ADR-3: Validate file types with an extension allowlist plus `file-type` sniffing of the head buffer, using an explicit table\n39\t\n40\t**Context.** FR-5 requires checks on both the extension and the magic number. FR-6 notes that txt/csv have no signature and that docx/xlsx/pptx can sniff as `zip`. FR-41 allows SVG only for `logo`, and SVG is text, so file-type cannot detect it reliably. The Vitest environment is `jsdom`, so file-type's `\"node\"` export condition may not resolve there, while `fileTypeFromBuffer` exists in both the node and core entries.\n41\t\n42\t**Decision.** In `src/server/files/validate.ts`, keep one `ALLOWED: Record<ext, { mimes: string[]; sniff: 'required' | 'none' | 'zip-or-ooxml' }>` table:\n43\t- Binary types (pdf, doc, xls, ppt, png, jpg/jpeg, gif, webp, mp3, mp4, zip): the sniffed mime must be in `mimes`. doc/xls/ppt sniff as `application/x-cfb`.\n44\t- docx/xlsx/pptx: the sniffed type is `application/zip` or the matching OOXML mime.\n45\t- txt/csv and svg (logo only): sniffing must return `undefined`. If any signature is detected (for example an ELF, PE or zip disguised as .csv), the file is rejected. SVG also requires the head to contain `<svg`, and is limited to 2 MB under context `logo` (FR-40). The logo context also limits extensions to png, jpg, jpeg, webp and svg.\n46\t- The stored `mimeType` comes from the table, not from the client's Content-Type header.\n47\t- Call `fileTypeFromBuffer(head)` on the first 4100 bytes collected from the stream. `head` is a tee of the first chunks, not a re-read.\n48\t\n49\t**Consequences.** The rules are deterministic and testable as a pure function (FR-48). Integration tests that touch busboy or the filesystem add `// @vitest-environment node`. Errors use the FR-5 message `File type .<ext> is not allowed.`\n50\t\n51\t---\n52\t\n53\t## ADR-4: Store files on local disk outside `public/`, serve them only through a Node route handler, and set headers per response\n54\t\n55\t**Context.** NFR-1 says files are never served from a static path. FR-8 sets nosniff, attachment and inline rules. FR-41 sets a CSP for SVG logos. FR-10 requires `logo` to be readable without a session. `src/middleware.ts` returns 401 for any `/api/` path that is not in `PUBLIC_API_PATHS` (`src/config/public-routes.ts`). `next.config.js` already sends a site-wide `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` and a CSP of `default-src 'self'` on `/:path*`.\n56\t\n57\t**Decision.**\n58\t- `src/server/storage.ts` uses `node:fs/promises` and `node:stream/promises` only. `UPLOAD_DIR` is resolved with `path.resolve` and defaults to `./uploads`. Every storage key is checked to stay under `UPLOAD_DIR` after resolution, as a path-traversal guard. This is the only implementation (NFR-11).\n59\t- `GET /api/files/:id` runs on `runtime = 'nodejs'` and returns `new Response(Readable.toWeb(fs.createReadStream(...)))` with `Content-Type` set to the stored mime, `Content-Length` set to `sizeBytes`, `nosniff`, and `Content-Disposition` set to `attachment` (or `inline` only for image/* or pdf with `?inline=1`) plus an RFC 5987 `filename*`. SVG responses also set `Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'unsafe-inline'`. If next.config's global CSP is also sent, browsers enforce the stricter intersection, so no config change is needed for this route.\n60\t- Add `'/api/files/'` to `PUBLIC_API_PATHS` so unauthenticated logo fetches reach the handler. The handler then runs the access registry (FR-9) for every request: `auth()` returns `null` for anonymous users, and only the `logo` resolver accepts a null user. Every other context returns 404 without reading bytes (NFR-8).\n61\t- `.gitignore`: create one at the repo root containing `uploads/`, plus `node_modules`, `.next` and `.env` if missing. `.env.example` documents `UPLOAD_DIR` and `MAX_UPLOAD_MB` (NFR-12).\n62\t\n63\t**Consequences.** Opening the middleware gate moves the auth decision into the handler, so the access registry must deny by default (FR-9: an unknown context is owner/ADMIN only, and an anonymous user is denied). Integration tests must cover anonymous requests for a lesson file (expect 404) and for a logo (expect 200).\n64\t\n65\t---\n66\t\n67\t## ADR-5: Allow the video-embed origins in the site CSP via `frame-src`\n68\t\n69\t**Context.** FR-30 embeds `youtube-nocookie.com/embed/<id>` and `player.vimeo.com/video/<id>` iframes. The current CSP has `default-src 'self'` and no `frame-src`, so the browser would block both embeds.\n70\t\n71\t**Decision.** Add `frame-src https://www.youtube-nocookie.com https://player.vimeo.com;` to the CSP string in `next.config.js`. The URL parser lives in `src/lib/video.ts` as a pure function shared by the Zod validator (FR-16) and the viewer (FR-32). It only emits those two origins.\n72\t\n73\t**Consequences.** This is a one-line config change. The `X-Frame-Options: DENY` header only stops other sites from framing SchoolHub, so it does not affect these embeds. Playwright should check that the iframe loads (it is not CSP-blocked) on the seeded YouTube lesson (FR-47).\n74\t\n75\t---\n76\t\n77\t## ADR-6: Render Markdown with react-markdown, remark-gfm and rehype-sanitize, map every element to MUI, and restrict images to same origin\n78\t\n79\t**Context.** FR-31 and NFR-2 require sanitised Markdown with `img` allowed from same origin only and MUI element mapping (NFR-7). The current CSP has `img-src 'self' data:`.\n80\t\n81\t**Decision.** `src/components/MarkdownView/MarkdownView.tsx` is a client-safe component (no hooks, so it also renders on the server) with `remarkPlugins={[remarkGfm]}` and `rehypePlugins={[[rehypeSanitize, schema]]}`. `schema` extends `defaultSchema` and limits `img` `src` to relative paths: `protocols.src: []` plus a check that the value starts with `/`. The default schema already drops `javascript:` hrefs. A `components` map converts h1–h6, p, a, ul/ol/li, table/thead/tbody/tr/th/td, hr, blockquote, code and pre into MUI Typography, Link, List/ListItem, Table*, Divider and `Box component=\"pre\"`. External `a` elements get `target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"`. The editor Preview tab (FR-25) uses the same component.\n82\t\n83\t**Consequences.** One renderer is used everywhere, so a sanitisation regression shows up in one unit test with `<script>`, `javascript:` and off-origin `img` cases (FR-48). No CSP change is needed for images.\n84\t\n85\t---\n86\t\n87\t## ADR-7: Implement reordering with @dnd-kit/sortable, persist the whole order in one transaction, and provide keyboard IconButtons\n88\t\n89\t**Context.** FR-23 covers drag within and across modules, with the full ordering sent to `POST /api/courses/:id/content/reorder`. NFR-6 requires a keyboard alternative. @dnd-kit/core is already installed.\n90\t\n91\t**Decision.** Each module's lesson List is a `SortableContext` with `verticalListSortingStrategy`, all inside one `DndContext`, and cross-container moves are handled in `onDragOver`. Up/down `IconButton`s modify the same client order array. The server validates that every submitted module and lesson id belongs to the course and that the set equals the existing set. It then writes `position`/`moduleId` for all rows inside one `prisma.$transaction`. A course has at most about 200 lessons (NFR-4), so updating every row is acceptable.\n92\t\n93\t**Consequences.** Reorder is a single idempotent call with no partial states. The update count is O(n) per reorder, which is fine at this scale.\n94\t\n95\t---\n96\t\n97\t## ADR-8: Read content with a bounded number of Prisma queries and compute visibility in TypeScript\n98\t\n99\t**Context.** NFR-4 requires the Content tab to render 20×10 lessons in under 600 ms with no N+1 queries. FR-18 evaluates visibility at read time, with no background job.\n100\t\n101\t**Decision.** `GET /api/courses/:id/modules` and the Content tab server component call `content-service.listCourseContent(user, courseId)`. That function makes one `module.findMany` with `include: { lessons: { include: { _count: { select: { attachments: true } } } } }` ordered by position, plus one `lessonProgress.findMany` for the student's lessons in that course when the caller is a STUDENT. That is two queries in total. `isVisible(entity, now)` is a pure function in `src/server/content/visibility.ts` and is unit tested (FR-48). Both module and lesson must be visible. Add an `@@index([courseId, position])` on Module and an `@@index([moduleId, position])` on Lesson.\n102\t\n103\t**Consequences.** Visibility is not pushed into SQL. At 200 rows per course, filtering in memory costs nothing, and having the rule in one place means the viewer (FR-32), the Learning tab (FR-35) and FR-44 all agree.\n104\t\n105\t---\n106\t\n107\t## ADR-9: Use existing stack pieces for scheduling, timezones, autosave and notifications\n108\t\n109\t**Context.** FR-28, NFR-10, FR-29, FR-38/39 and FR-12 all match existing infrastructure.\n110\t\n111\t**Decision.**\n112\t- Schedule picker: use the `DateTimePicker` from `@mui/x-date-pickers` (already wrapped in `src/components/FormField`). Convert school-timezone input to UTC with `date-fns-tz` `fromZonedTime(SchoolSettings.timezone)`. Zod rejects `publishAt <= now` (FR-28, NFR-10).\n113\t- Autosave (FR-29): a `setInterval` of 10 s plus a dirty flag in the editor, a `beforeunload` listener, and `PATCH /api/lessons/:id`. No new library.\n114\t- Notifications: add `content.lesson_published` to `NOTIFICATION_TYPES` and call `notify()` only from the publish-now path (FR-38, FR-39).\n115\t- Orphan purge (FR-12): call `purgeOrphanFiles()` next to `purgeOldNotifications()` inside the existing `lastPurgeAt` daily block in `src/app/api/internal/email/dispatch/route.ts`.\n116\t- Logging (NFR-12): use `logger` from `src/lib/logger.ts`. Rate limit (NFR-3): use the existing `rateLimit()`, which is per-process. That limitation is already marked with a ponytail comment.\n117\t\n118\t**Consequences.** No scheduler, queue or cron is added. Scheduled lessons appear automatically when read after `publishAt`, and no notification is sent for them, as FR-39 requires.\n119\t\n120\t---\n121\t\n122\t## Open risks carried forward\n123\t1. **NFR-7:** `mui-mcp` tools (`useMuiDocs`/`fetchDocs`) were visible in this session's tool list. Dev must still look up every MUI component against 9.5.0 before using it. If the server is unreachable, that is a hard blocker.\n124\t2. **ADR-2:** depending on Next's transitive busboy is a small fragility (see the Consequences of ADR-2).\n125\t3. **FR-40/42 (LOW confidence):** logo URLs stored as `/api/files/<id>` need an `APP_URL` prefix in email templates. The new `/admin/settings/school` page is a minimal page built on the existing `PATCH /api/settings/school`.\n126\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"34574e28-0591-42df-96fb-64403ec6dad4","uuid":"747f6f3e-949d-452c-9e2d-a30de46ad520","timestamp":"2026-11-06T21:28:36.846Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/ikramaansari/Documents/forge/schoolhub/artifacts/architect/tech-stack-decisions.md","content":"# Tech Stack Decisions: PRD-08 File Storage & Course Content (SCH-008)\n\nTicket: SH-8 · Phase: architect (arch-research-stack) · Timestamp: 2026-11-06T06:30:00Z · Status: complete\n\n**Base stack.** `artifacts/intake/tech-stack.json` reports `react` at 100% confidence, and the workspace confirms it: Next.js 14.2.18 App Router, React 18.3.1, TypeScript strict (`moduleResolution: bundler`), Prisma 5.20.0 on PostgreSQL 16, Auth.js v5 beta.25, Zod 3.23.8, MUI 9.5.0 (material, icons-material, material-nextjs, x-data-grid, x-date-pickers with `AdapterDateFns` already in `src/app/providers.tsx`, x-charts), @dnd-kit/core 6.3.1, date-fns/date-fns-tz, Vitest 2.1.1 (jsdom) and Playwright 1.47.2. `package.json` declares `engines.node >=18.18.0`, and local Node is 24.11.1. Nothing in the requirements calls for a different stack. The ADRs below cover how this feature fits into the existing stack and pin the new dependencies.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-1: Keep the existing stack and add only the five PRD-allowed dependencies, with pinned versions\n\n**Context.** NFR-9 limits new dependencies to file-type, react-markdown, remark-gfm, rehype-sanitize and @dnd-kit/sortable. The latest file-type (22.x) declares `engines.node >=22` and 21.x declares `>=20`. Either would break the repo's `>=18.18.0` engine contract. All five packages are ESM-only.\n\n**Decision.** Install these exact pins:\n| Package | Version | Why this version |\n|---|---|---|\n| `file-type` | `20.5.0` | Newest line with `engines.node >=18`. Use `fileTypeFromBuffer` (FR-5, FR-6). |\n| `react-markdown` | `10.1.0` | Peer is `react >=18` (FR-31). |\n| `remark-gfm` | `4.0.1` | Supports tables and strikethrough for FR-25 and FR-31. |\n| `rehype-sanitize` | `6.0.0` | Provides NFR-2 sanitisation. |\n| `@dnd-kit/sortable` | `10.0.0` | Peer is `@dnd-kit/core ^6.3.0`, which matches the installed 6.3.1 (FR-23). |\n\nESM-only packages import cleanly into App Router server and client code under `moduleResolution: bundler`, so `transpilePackages` is not needed. Add it only if `next build` complains.\n\n**Consequences.** No engine bump is needed. Do not upgrade file-type past 20.x without also raising `engines.node`. NFR-11 (single local-disk storage, no S3 SDK) means no cloud dependency is added.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-2: Parse multipart uploads with `busboy` (already installed via Next.js), not `request.formData()`\n\n**Context.** NFR-5 requires uploads to stream to disk, with sha256 computed and the size limit enforced during the stream. In Next 14, `Request.formData()` buffers the whole body in memory, which violates NFR-5 for 25 MB files (FR-4). `busboy@1.6.0` is already in `node_modules` as a direct dependency of `next@14.2.18` (`npm ls busboy`). Adding another parser would break NFR-9.\n\n**Decision.** `POST /api/files` (FR-3) runs on the Node runtime (`export const runtime = 'nodejs'`). It pipes `Readable.fromWeb(req.body)` into `busboy({ headers, limits: { files: 1, fileSize: MAX_UPLOAD_MB*1024*1024 } })`. The file stream goes through a `crypto.createHash('sha256')` tap and into `storage.saveFile()` (FR-2), which writes to a temp path under `UPLOAD_DIR` and then `rename`s it to `<yyyy>/<mm>/<cuid>.<ext>` (FR-1). Busboy's `limit` event aborts the upload, deletes the temp file and returns `VALIDATION_ERROR 'File exceeds 25 MB.'` (FR-4). Type sniffing (ADR-3) runs on the first chunk(s) before the write is committed. The `context` field must come before the file part in the multipart body because the logo rules (FR-40, FR-41) depend on it. FileUpload (FR-13) appends `context` first.\n\n**Consequences.** busboy is a transitive dependency, so `@types/busboy` is not available. Declare a minimal local type or accept `any` at that one call site, with a `ponytail:` comment explaining it. If a Next upgrade drops busboy, add `busboy` explicitly at that point. The intake handoff should note this as the only deviation from the dependency list, and it is a zero-install one. The rate limit check (NFR-3, `rateLimit('upload:'+userId, 60, 3_600_000)` from `src/server/rate-limit.ts`) runs before any bytes are read.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-3: Validate file types with an extension allowlist plus `file-type` sniffing of the head buffer, using an explicit table\n\n**Context.** FR-5 requires checks on both the extension and the magic number. FR-6 notes that txt/csv have no signature and that docx/xlsx/pptx can sniff as `zip`. FR-41 allows SVG only for `logo`, and SVG is text, so file-type cannot detect it reliably. The Vitest environment is `jsdom`, so file-type's `\"node\"` export condition may not resolve there, while `fileTypeFromBuffer` exists in both the node and core entries.\n\n**Decision.** In `src/server/files/validate.ts`, keep one `ALLOWED: Record<ext, { mimes: string[]; sniff: 'required' | 'none' | 'zip-or-ooxml' }>` table:\n- Binary types (pdf, doc, xls, ppt, png, jpg/jpeg, gif, webp, mp3, mp4, zip): the sniffed mime must be in `mimes`. doc/xls/ppt sniff as `application/x-cfb`.\n- docx/xlsx/pptx: the sniffed type is `application/zip` or the matching OOXML mime.\n- txt/csv and svg (logo only): sniffing must return `undefined`. If any signature is detected (for example an ELF, PE or zip disguised as .csv), the file is rejected. SVG also requires the head to contain `<svg`, and is limited to 2 MB under context `logo` (FR-40). The logo context also limits extensions to png, jpg, jpeg, webp and svg.\n- The stored `mimeType` comes from the table, not from the client's Content-Type header.\n- Call `fileTypeFromBuffer(head)` on the first 4100 bytes collected from the stream. `head` is a tee of the first chunks, not a re-read.\n\n**Consequences.** The rules are deterministic and testable as a pure function (FR-48). Integration tests that touch busboy or the filesystem add `// @vitest-environment node`. Errors use the FR-5 message `File type .<ext> is not allowed.`\n\n---\n\n## ADR-4: Store files on local disk outside `public/`, serve them only through a Node route handler, and set headers per response\n\n**Context.** NFR-1 says files are never served from a static path. FR-8 sets nosniff, attachment and inline rules. FR-41 sets a CSP for SVG logos. FR-10 requires `logo` to be readable without a session. `src/middleware.ts` returns 401 for any `/api/` path that is not in `PUBLIC_API_PATHS` (`src/config/public-routes.ts`). `next.config.js` already sends a site-wide `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` and a CSP of `default-src 'self'` on `/:path*`.\n\n**Decision.**\n- `src/server/storage.ts` uses `node:fs/promises` and `node:stream/promises` only. `UPLOAD_DIR` is resolved with `path.resolve` and defaults to `./uploads`. Every storage key is checked to stay under `UPLOAD_DIR` after resolution, as a path-traversal guard. This is the only implementation (NFR-11).\n- `GET /api/files/:id` runs on `runtime = 'nodejs'` and returns `new Response(Readable.toWeb(fs.createReadStream(...)))` with `Content-Type` set to the stored mime, `Content-Length` set to `sizeBytes`, `nosniff`, and `Content-Disposition` set to `attachment` (or `inline` only for image/* or pdf with `?inline=1`) plus an RFC 5987 `filename*`. SVG responses also set `Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'none'; style-src 'unsafe-inline'`. If next.config's global CSP is also sent, browsers enforce the stricter intersection, so no config change is needed for this route.\n- Add `'/api/files/'` to `PUBLIC_API_PATHS` so unauthenticated logo fetches reach the handler. The handler then runs the access registry (FR-9) for every request: `auth()` returns `null` for anonymous users, and only the `logo` resolver accepts a null user. Every other context returns 404 without reading bytes (NFR-8).\n- `.gitignore`: create one at the repo root containing `uploads/`, plus `node_modules`, `.next` and `.env` if missing. `.env.example` documents `UPLOAD_DIR` and `MAX_UPLOAD_MB` (NFR-12).\n\n**Consequences.** Opening the middleware gate moves the auth decision into the handler, so the access registry must deny by default (FR-9: an unknown context is owner/ADMIN only, and an anonymous user is denied). Integration tests must cover anonymous requests for a lesson file (expect 404) and for a logo (expect 200).\n\n---\n\n## ADR-5: Allow the video-embed origins in the site CSP via `frame-src`\n\n**Context.** FR-30 embeds `youtube-nocookie.com/embed/<id>` and `player.vimeo.com/video/<id>` iframes. The current CSP has `default-src 'self'` and no `frame-src`, so the browser would block both embeds.\n\n**Decision.** Add `frame-src https://www.youtube-nocookie.com https://player.vimeo.com;` to the CSP string in `next.config.js`. The URL parser lives in `src/lib/video.ts` as a pure function shared by the Zod validator (FR-16) and the viewer (FR-32). It only emits those two origins.\n\n**Consequences.** This is a one-line config change. The `X-Frame-Options: DENY` header only stops other sites from framing SchoolHub, so it does not affect these embeds. Playwright should check that the iframe loads (it is not CSP-blocked) on the seeded YouTube lesson (FR-47).\n\n---\n\n## ADR-6: Render Markdown with react-markdown, remark-gfm and rehype-sanitize, map every element to MUI, and restrict images to same origin\n\n**Context.** FR-31 and NFR-2 require sanitised Markdown with `img` allowed from same origin only and MUI element mapping (NFR-7). The current CSP has `img-src 'self' data:`.\n\n**Decision.** `src/components/MarkdownView/MarkdownView.tsx` is a client-safe component (no hooks, so it also renders on the server) with `remarkPlugins={[remarkGfm]}` and `rehypePlugins={[[rehypeSanitize, schema]]}`. `schema` extends `defaultSchema` and limits `img` `src` to relative paths: `protocols.src: []` plus a check that the value starts with `/`. The default schema already drops `javascript:` hrefs. A `components` map converts h1–h6, p, a, ul/ol/li, table/thead/tbody/tr/th/td, hr, blockquote, code and pre into MUI Typography, Link, List/ListItem, Table*, Divider and `Box component=\"pre\"`. External `a` elements get `target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"`. The editor Preview tab (FR-25) uses the same component.\n\n**Consequences.** One renderer is used everywhere, so a sanitisation regression shows up in one unit test with `<script>`, `javascript:` and off-origin `img` cases (FR-48). No CSP change is needed for images.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-7: Implement reordering with @dnd-kit/sortable, persist the whole order in one transaction, and provide keyboard IconButtons\n\n**Context.** FR-23 covers drag within and across modules, with the full ordering sent to `POST /api/courses/:id/content/reorder`. NFR-6 requires a keyboard alternative. @dnd-kit/core is already installed.\n\n**Decision.** Each module's lesson List is a `SortableContext` with `verticalListSortingStrategy`, all inside one `DndContext`, and cross-container moves are handled in `onDragOver`. Up/down `IconButton`s modify the same client order array. The server validates that every submitted module and lesson id belongs to the course and that the set equals the existing set. It then writes `position`/`moduleId` for all rows inside one `prisma.$transaction`. A course has at most about 200 lessons (NFR-4), so updating every row is acceptable.\n\n**Consequences.** Reorder is a single idempotent call with no partial states. The update count is O(n) per reorder, which is fine at this scale.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-8: Read content with a bounded number of Prisma queries and compute visibility in TypeScript\n\n**Context.** NFR-4 requires the Content tab to render 20×10 lessons in under 600 ms with no N+1 queries. FR-18 evaluates visibility at read time, with no background job.\n\n**Decision.** `GET /api/courses/:id/modules` and the Content tab server component call `content-service.listCourseContent(user, courseId)`. That function makes one `module.findMany` with `include: { lessons: { include: { _count: { select: { attachments: true } } } } }` ordered by position, plus one `lessonProgress.findMany` for the student's lessons in that course when the caller is a STUDENT. That is two queries in total. `isVisible(entity, now)` is a pure function in `src/server/content/visibility.ts` and is unit tested (FR-48). Both module and lesson must be visible. Add an `@@index([courseId, position])` on Module and an `@@index([moduleId, position])` on Lesson.\n\n**Consequences.** Visibility is not pushed into SQL. At 200 rows per course, filtering in memory costs nothing, and having the rule in one place means the viewer (FR-32), the Learning tab (FR-35) and FR-44 all agree.\n\n---\n\n## ADR-9: Use existing stack pieces for scheduling, timezones, autosave and notifications\n\n**Context.** FR-28, NFR-10, FR-29, FR-38/39 and FR-12 all match existing infrastructure.\n\n**Decision.**\n- Schedule picker: use the `DateTimePicker` from `@mui/x-date-pickers` (already wrapped in `src/components/FormField`). Convert school-timezone input to UTC with `date-fns-tz` `fromZonedTime(SchoolSettings.timezone)`. Zod rejects `publishAt <= now` (FR-28, NFR-10).\n- Autosave (FR-29): a `setInterval` of 10 s plus a dirty flag in the editor, a `beforeunload` listener, and `PATCH /api/lessons/:id`. No new library.\n- Notifications: add `content.lesson_published` to `NOTIFICATION_TYPES` and call `notify()` only from the publish-now path (FR-38, FR-39).\n- Orphan purge (FR-12): call `purgeOrphanFiles()` next to `purgeOldNotifications()` inside the existing `lastPurgeAt` daily block in `src/app/api/internal/email/dispatch/route.ts`.\n- Logging (NFR-12): use `logger` from `src/lib/logger.ts`. Rate limit (NFR-3): use the existing `rateLimit()`, which is per-process. That limitation is already marked with a ponytail comment.\n\n**Consequences.** No scheduler, queue or cron is added. Scheduled lessons appear automatically when read after `publishAt`, and no notification is sent for them, as FR-39 requires.\n\n---\n\n## Open risks carried forward\n1. **NFR-7:** `mui-mcp` tools (`useMuiDocs`/`fetchDocs`) were visible in this session's tool list. Dev must still look up every MUI component against 9.5.0 before using it. If the server is unreachable, that is a hard blocker.\n2. **ADR-2:** depending on Next's transitive busboy is a small fragility (see the Consequences of ADR-2).\n3. **FR-40/42 (LOW confidence):** logo URLs stored as `/api/files/<id>` need an `APP_URL` prefix in email templates. The new `/admin/settings/school` page is a minimal page built on the existing `PATCH /api/settings/school`.\n","numLines":126,"startLine":1,"totalLines":126}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_019hFysH5tTDETz4SG6xdXC8","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
